    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d33">

                <pb facs="0105" n="21" sameAs="frus:frus1918Supp02/pg21"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">File No. 763.72114/3267</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in Denmark</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Grant-Smith</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Telegram]</seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Copenhagen</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1918-02-03T17:00:00+01:00">
                            <hi rendition="#italic">February 3, 1918, 5 p.m.</hi>
                        </date>
                        <lb/> [<hi rendition="#italic">Received February 4, 6.50
                        p.m.</hi>]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1906. Department’s unnumbered [<hi rendition="#italic">668</hi>], January
                        8.<anchor n="1" xml:id="d33-f1"/> Danish Government agrees to the free
                    transmission through Denmark of postal matter destined for prisoners of war.
                    Text follows: <quote rend="blockquote">
                        <p>Monsieur le Chargé d’Affaires: Referring to the note of January 9, 1918,
                            No. 773, I have honor to communicate that pursuant to transport rules
                            laid down in the Hague regulations of 1907, article 16, and in the
                            universal postal convention of 1906, article 2 [<hi rendition="#italic"
                                >11</hi>], section 4, and in accordance with point of view taken by
                            the Royal Danish Government in equal matters, all letters, money orders
                            and objects of value as well as parcels destined for or in camps of
                            prisoners or camps of concentration will be transmitted postage free <pb
                                facs="0106" n="22" xml:id="pg22"/> to their destination. I avail
                            myself of this opportunity to renew to you, Monsieur le Chargé
                            d’Affaires, the assurance of my high consideration. Signed Erik Scavenius.</p>
                    </quote>
                </p>
                <closer>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">American Legation</hi>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
